WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.650
This is the Great Barrier
Reef, and it's dying.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.987
Less than 20 years ago, the
world's largest living structure

00:00:07.987 --> 00:00:11.057
looked like this, and this.

00:00:11.057 --> 00:00:13.726
But today, ongoing pressures
from climate change

00:00:13.726 --> 00:00:17.229
put it at serious risk for
being wiped out entirely.

00:00:17.229 --> 00:00:19.732
In fact, in August, an official report

00:00:19.732 --> 00:00:21.067
by the Australian government

00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:22.902
downgraded the reef's future outlook

00:00:22.902 --> 00:00:25.771
from "poor" to "very poor."

00:00:25.771 --> 00:00:28.274
So, how exactly did we get here?

00:00:28.274 --> 00:00:29.775
And can it be reversed?

00:00:33.245 --> 00:00:35.114
The Great Barrier Reef is big.

00:00:35.114 --> 00:00:38.317
So big, in fact, that you
can see it from space.

00:00:39.452 --> 00:00:41.053
David Wachenfeld: It's
important for people to remember

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:43.055
that the Great Barrier Reef is much more

00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:44.557
than just one coral reef,

00:00:44.557 --> 00:00:46.959
which is what I think
a lot of people think.

00:00:46.959 --> 00:00:50.863
So, it's actually 3,000
separate, individual coral reefs,

00:00:50.863 --> 00:00:53.633
and every one of them is
in a different condition.

00:00:53.633 --> 00:00:56.302
Narrator: It's also the world's
largest living structure

00:00:56.302 --> 00:00:59.872
as well as a home to thousands
of plant and animal species.

00:00:59.872 --> 00:01:01.907
Jessica Carilli: Like
with trees in a forest,

00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:04.610
the corals are really the
foundation of the habitat.

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.545
They provide the structure

00:01:06.545 --> 00:01:08.514
for other organisms to live there.

00:01:08.514 --> 00:01:12.818
A normal, healthy coral
reef is very colorful.

00:01:12.818 --> 00:01:16.222
There's tons of fish swimming around.

00:01:16.222 --> 00:01:18.124
It's often quite loud underwater

00:01:18.124 --> 00:01:20.693
when you're diving on a
coral reef that's healthy.

00:01:20.693 --> 00:01:23.562
So, you know, it's just
amazing and beautiful.

00:01:23.562 --> 00:01:24.997
Narrator: But this vast ecosystem

00:01:24.997 --> 00:01:26.866
has been declining for a while.

00:01:26.866 --> 00:01:29.502
The culprit? Mass coral bleaching.

00:01:30.403 --> 00:01:33.472
As the name suggests, coral
bleaching strips reefs

00:01:33.472 --> 00:01:36.642
of their color, leaving
them completely white.

00:01:37.777 --> 00:01:40.446
It happens when unnaturally
warm ocean water

00:01:40.446 --> 00:01:43.582
causes coral to expel the
symbiotic, colorful algae

00:01:43.582 --> 00:01:45.084
it needs to survive.

00:01:45.084 --> 00:01:47.119
Without this algae, the coral can't get

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:49.188
the nutrients it needs
and will starve to death

00:01:49.188 --> 00:01:51.791
if water temperatures
don't return to normal.

00:01:51.791 --> 00:01:54.593
Carilli: Even small
increases in temperature,

00:01:54.593 --> 00:01:56.729
when they're sustained for a long period,

00:01:56.729 --> 00:01:59.131
can be very stressful for them.

00:01:59.131 --> 00:02:00.433
It's just like a person, right?

00:02:00.433 --> 00:02:04.303
Like, you can go out
into 114-degree weather

00:02:04.303 --> 00:02:06.338
for a short time and you'll be fine,

00:02:06.338 --> 00:02:07.840
but if you were to stay out there all day,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:10.309
you would suffer badly.

00:02:10.309 --> 00:02:13.345
Narrator: In 2016 and 2017,

00:02:13.345 --> 00:02:14.880
the Great Barrier Reef was hit

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.116
with a massive marine heat wave,

00:02:17.116 --> 00:02:21.053
leading to the longest
coral-bleaching events ever recorded.

00:02:21.053 --> 00:02:25.691
It devastated the reef, killing
off nearly 50% of the coral.

00:02:25.691 --> 00:02:29.061
Carilli: Many corals can
live for hundreds of years,

00:02:29.061 --> 00:02:31.597
so if those corals die,

00:02:31.597 --> 00:02:35.701
to get back a coral that
was 400 years old and died

00:02:35.701 --> 00:02:38.270
is gonna take 400 years.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.139
Narrator: But here's the thing:

00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:42.875
This rate of bleaching
is not only abnormal

00:02:42.875 --> 00:02:44.577
but completely unprecedented

00:02:44.577 --> 00:02:46.712
in the Great Barrier Reef's history.

00:02:46.712 --> 00:02:48.848
Wachenfeld: We have to remember
that the Great Barrier Reef

00:02:48.848 --> 00:02:51.417
is now essentially a changed ecosystem.

00:02:51.417 --> 00:02:53.853
We've never had events as severe

00:02:53.853 --> 00:02:57.189
as those of 2016 and '17 before.

00:02:57.189 --> 00:02:59.992
The cumulative consequence
of all of that is that

00:02:59.992 --> 00:03:02.561
we don't know how well
the Great Barrier Reef

00:03:02.561 --> 00:03:05.364
is going to recover from those events.

00:03:05.364 --> 00:03:07.299
Narrator: Before the
1980s, bleaching events

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:10.736
would occur periodically,
but never on a mass scale.

00:03:10.736 --> 00:03:14.306
But global warming has rapidly
accelerated this process.

00:03:14.306 --> 00:03:17.009
Carilli: From those coral
skeletal records, we know

00:03:17.009 --> 00:03:22.014
conclusively that coral
bleaching is a new phenomenon.

00:03:22.648 --> 00:03:24.483
The first mass-bleaching event

00:03:24.483 --> 00:03:27.953
that occurred on the Great
Barrier Reef was in 1982.

00:03:27.953 --> 00:03:30.923
As bleaching events are
becoming more severe

00:03:30.923 --> 00:03:35.127
and more frequent, it
stymies the recovery, right?

00:03:35.127 --> 00:03:37.463
It's almost as if you have a hurricane,

00:03:37.463 --> 00:03:39.799
you start to rebuild, and then
you get hit by another one.

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:42.201
Narrator: In fact, a 2019 report

00:03:42.201 --> 00:03:43.736
by the United Nations warned

00:03:43.736 --> 00:03:45.638
that if global temperatures increased

00:03:45.638 --> 00:03:50.242
by just 0.9 degrees Celsius,
which is expected to happen,

00:03:50.242 --> 00:03:53.979
coral reefs could decline by 70% to 90%.

00:03:53.979 --> 00:03:56.382
And if it warms by 1.8 degrees,

00:03:56.382 --> 00:03:59.885
99% of the world's coral could be at risk.

00:03:59.885 --> 00:04:02.021
Wachenfeld: As we approach
2 degrees of warming

00:04:02.021 --> 00:04:04.290
and go beyond 2 degrees of warming,

00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.125
we are probably not going to be able

00:04:06.125 --> 00:04:08.027
to protect the Great Barrier Reef anymore,

00:04:08.027 --> 00:04:09.428
and we're actually going to lose it

00:04:09.428 --> 00:04:11.997
as the valuable ecosystem that we have.

00:04:11.997 --> 00:04:13.432
Narrator: As if that wasn't enough,

00:04:13.432 --> 00:04:16.101
the reef is also endangered
by local pollution,

00:04:16.101 --> 00:04:18.537
overfishing, and coastal development.

00:04:19.505 --> 00:04:22.341
Carilli: Local management of impacts,

00:04:22.341 --> 00:04:26.011
such as pollution in runoff,

00:04:26.011 --> 00:04:27.980
sedimentation in our reefs,

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:31.951
overfishing, these things
are critically important,

00:04:31.951 --> 00:04:33.419
and they need to happen

00:04:33.419 --> 00:04:37.556
in concert with combating climate change.

00:04:37.556 --> 00:04:38.958
Narrator: It's also important to note

00:04:38.958 --> 00:04:40.392
that the Great Barrier Reef is one

00:04:40.392 --> 00:04:42.995
of the best-managed reefs in the world,

00:04:42.995 --> 00:04:44.864
but even with the best countermeasures,

00:04:44.864 --> 00:04:47.032
the Great Barrier Reef
simply can't withstand

00:04:47.032 --> 00:04:48.868
the effects of climate change.

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:51.303
Unless drastic international
action is taken

00:04:51.303 --> 00:04:54.073
to reverse or halt
greenhouse gas emissions,

00:04:54.073 --> 00:04:58.010
we might be facing a world
without reefs as early as 2050.

00:04:58.010 --> 00:05:00.846
Carilli: My ballpark is that
if we don't do something

00:05:00.846 --> 00:05:03.015
dramatic in the next 10 years,

00:05:03.015 --> 00:05:05.951
that we will have passed the time

00:05:05.951 --> 00:05:08.787
after which there will be no return.

00:05:09.722 --> 00:05:12.791
Narrator: So, this all sounds pretty bad,

00:05:12.791 --> 00:05:14.560
but why should we care?

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:16.962
What makes coral reefs so important?

00:05:18.631 --> 00:05:20.199
First of all, coral reefs are

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:22.401
an incredible source of biodiversity.

00:05:22.401 --> 00:05:27.006
In fact, they provide a home
for 25% of all marine life

00:05:27.006 --> 00:05:30.309
even though they take up
just 1% of the ocean floor.

00:05:30.309 --> 00:05:32.978
Wachenfeld: So, we have six
of the world's seven species

00:05:32.978 --> 00:05:37.149
of turtles, dugongs,
whales, dolphins, seabirds,

00:05:37.149 --> 00:05:39.685
one-and-a-half thousand species of fish.

00:05:39.685 --> 00:05:41.620
Narrator: They're also
worth billions of dollars

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:43.188
in economic value.

00:05:43.188 --> 00:05:44.790
The Great Barrier Reef alone

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:48.494
brings in over $6 billion
in tourism each year,

00:05:48.494 --> 00:05:52.665
and reefs provide a natural
buffer from violent storm waves,

00:05:52.665 --> 00:05:55.834
preventing property
damage and loss of life.

00:05:55.834 --> 00:05:58.003
Reefs are an increasingly
important resource

00:05:58.003 --> 00:06:00.706
for breakthrough medical treatments, too.

00:06:00.706 --> 00:06:03.375
Right now, plants and
animals found in coral reefs

00:06:03.375 --> 00:06:05.110
are being used to develop treatments

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.980
for diseases like cancer and Alzheimer's.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.549
Carilli: So, if we lose
corals, we potentially lose,

00:06:10.549 --> 00:06:14.920
you know, the key to solving
diseases that we care about.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.556
Narrator: Not to mention,
about half a billion people

00:06:17.556 --> 00:06:20.960
depend on reefs for food and work.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.395
Even though some of the
damage is irreversible,

00:06:23.395 --> 00:06:26.231
not all hope is lost for
the world's coral reefs.

00:06:27.266 --> 00:06:29.335
Carilli: We as a coral
reef science community

00:06:29.335 --> 00:06:33.472
are becoming increasingly
focused on human intervention

00:06:33.472 --> 00:06:36.542
and, you know, basically preventing

00:06:36.542 --> 00:06:39.345
the extinction of corals
and restoring reefs.

00:06:39.345 --> 00:06:41.814
The most important
thing that we need to do

00:06:41.814 --> 00:06:46.485
is to stop and reverse climate
change and global warming.

